Raster-Squall

Ingredients: PROGRESSIVE HOUSE + INDUSTRIAL TECHNO & SCHRANZ + HYPNOTIC, TRANCE-LIKE & MINIMAL + Clarinet
Creative Catalyst: The scene bloomed in a ghost mall where teenagers rewired abandoned arcade cabinets.

Raster-Squall is a brutalist, hyper-atmospheric collision where punishing 150-BPM industrial schranz drum kits slam against the cavernous, 64-bar builds of progressive house, all pierced by hyperventilating acoustic and contrabass clarinets routed directly through the FM sound chips and flyback transformers of gutted arcade cabinets.

Genre DNA

*   Tempo & Pulse: 148–156 BPM. A concussive, syncopated four-on-the-floor kick—slammed through arcade cabinet preamps until it squares off—is counterbalanced by a fluid, undulating progressive house sub-bass that snakes beneath the clatter like pressurized freon.
*   Harmonic Palette: Cold Aeolian and Dorian modes interwoven with microtonal woodwind overblowing; long-tail minor-ninth and suspended-fourth chord suspensions that hang suspended for minutes, evoking dead mall atriums and phosphorescent monitor glare.
*   Production Signatures: 
       Cathode-Gate Ducking:* The master kick drum triggers severe sidechain compression keyed to the 15kHz flyback transformer whine of scavenged CRT monitors, causing pads to breathe with electrical convulsions.
       Coin-Op Bitcrushing:* Live woodwinds are passed through re-soldered Yamaha YM2610 soundchips salvaged from Neo-Geo boards, giving acoustic clarinet runs a gritty, aliased 4-bit bite before dissolving into cavernous retail-space decay.
       Atrium Bellows Automation:* Progressive house filter sweeps constructed not from synthesizers, but from 32-track clarinet overdubs dynamically mapped to automated bandpass filters and modulated room reflections.
*   Dynamics: Relentless tension engineering. Tracks frequently strip away the punishing industrial artillery for two-minute stretches of bare, hypnotic woodwind trance and flickering monitor drone, only to detonate back into high-velocity schranz loops with zero transition fills.

Scene & Origin Lore

Raster-Squall crawled out of "The Galleria at St. Jude," an abandoned three-tier shopping complex in the rust-glazed suburbs of Ohio that closed its doors in 2004. Runaway youth, latchkey bedroom producers, and renegade conservatory dropouts occupied the dry concrete fountain pits, running pirate power cables to the mall's dead arcade. They gutted defunct Time Crisis and Ridge Racer machines, hotwiring their tactile buttons into drum triggers and jamming contact-mic'd clarinets into coin acceptors to hijack their preamps. The scene's uniform is utilitarian post-mall salvage: security-guard windbreakers lined with space blankets, silver chainmail strung from arcade token rolls, and wooden clarinet barrels wrapped in copper ground-wire. Dance rituals resemble rhythmic hyperventilation; crowds cluster in tight circles around towering walls of glowing, green-tinted CRT monitors, stomping in unison on loose ceramic floor tiles to add physical acoustic resonance to the seismic bass kicks.

Typical Instruments:
*   Buffet Crampon Bb Soprano Clarinet and Leblanc Contrabass Clarinet (fitted with internal piezo barrel pickups and copper shielding)
*   Salvaged arcade FM soundchips (Yamaha YM2610, YM2151) soldered into Eurorack rigs
*   Roland TR-909 and Jomox XBase 09 (overdriven through arcade coin-chute switches)
*   Korg MS-20 (processing raw woodwind audio into sub-bass growls)
*   Contact microphones taped to escalator structural beams and empty mall skylights

